hi this is presh talwalkar  last week the ted ed youtube channel  presented the frog riddle which is a  variation of a classic probability  problem  however in telling the story they  included a slight detail which would  actually change the calculation  in this video i'm going to present what  i consider to be the correct calculation  i'm going to go over a simplified  version of the riddle with pertinent  details  i'm going to explain how it relates to a  classic probability problem and one of  its variations and i'm going to show why  the ted-ed video actually meant to do  one calculation but they presented a  story for another calculation  so here's the  riddle imagine you have a population of  frogs that are equally likely to be male  and female and you cannot visually tell  the difference between the genders  you see two frogs  what is the probability there is a  female in the pair of frogs under two  different circumstances  circumstance a  the pair of frogs contains a male frog  this is the calculation the ted-ed video  wanted to  present what the story actually  presented was variation b  the pear contains a male frog because  you heard a croak that you know is  distinctive to male frogs  it might seem to you that variations a  and b  are going to be the same in either case  you know there's one mil frog in the  pair  but the detail that you heard the croak  of the frog actually changes the  probability  so in order to explain why i'm going to  go over the classic probability problem  that this is based on  the boy or girl paradox  imagine mr jones has two children  you want to know the probability that he  has one girl  under two different variations  variation a he has a boy  variation b he has a boy born on tuesday  it seems like these two variations would  have the same probability  but let's go over the calculation to see  why it's actually different in a and b  so variation a  let's consider mr jones having two  children what's the sample space  we know that he could have two boys  we know he could have a boy and a girl  he could have a girl and a boy or he  could have two girls these are four  events and they're equally likely to  happen if we assume the probability of  having a boy and a girl is equal  if we know that he has a boy  the event that he has two girls is not  possible  so we have a new sample space  in which there are three different  events he has two boys a boy and a girl  or a girl and a boy and these are  equally likely events  what is the probability that he has a  girl in this new sample space  the events boy girl and girl boy are  equally likely  there are two events in which he has a  girl out of three  therefore the probability he has a girl  if we know he has a boy is equal to  two-thirds  now let's consider the other variation  what would happen if we know he has a  boy that's born on a tuesday now this  seems like it shouldn't affect the  calculation  knowing he has a boy obviously the boy  would have to be born on some day of the  week so whether he's born on tuesday or  any other day shouldn't matter  however the fact we learned that the boy  is born on a tuesday does change our  sample space  so we know there are only three possible  pairs of genders it could either be boy  boy boy girl or girl boy he can't have  two girls because we know that he has a  boy  we also know that the boy is born on a  tuesday so let's indicate the days of  the week by a numerical code zero  equaling sunday  one equaling monday and counting up all  the way until six equaling saturday  we'll write the day of the week that a  child is born using a subscript  so what is our sample space now  for boys that are boys and girls that  are born on different days of the week  we need to define the events that are  equally likely to happen  if he has two boys it's possible that  the first child was born on a tuesday  and the second child was equally likely  to be born on any of the other any of  the seven days of the week  it could also be that the older child is  born on a tuesday and the  the other child is born on any of the  days of the week equally likely to  happen  if he has a boy girl we know the boy has  to be born on a tuesday and the girl can  be born on any days of the week and  similarly for our last case  there's one thing to consider here  actually  we've written down  b2b2 twice  we need to remove this so that we're  only considering  equally likely events  we now have a list of 27 equally likely  events in our sample space  out of these  seven plus seven involve mr jones having  a girl  therefore we can calculate the  probability has a girl is 14 divided by  27 which is approximately 52 percent and  this is closer to our intuition that  when he has two children we suspect that  it's equally likely knowing that one is  a boy that the other could be a girl is  almost 50 percent  so now let's relate that to the ted ed  frog riddle  the video presented the calculation for  variation a  however  they actually present to the story of  variation b and that's going to be  similar to the boy or girl paradox  knowing that the boy was born on a  tuesday so let's go through this  calculation  we know that if we have two frogs  they can either be male male male female  female male or female female  if we know that there is a male frog in  the pair  we remove the female female pair and we  end up with a new sample space of three  equally likely events male male male  female and female male  in two of these events  there is a female  so we can calculate the probability  there's a female is equal to two divided  by three  now this is the probability that's  presented in the ted ed video and it  would be true if you knew that in a pair  of frogs there's a male frog you learn  that by random chance  however the ted-ed video presented the  variation that you know that there's a  male frog because you heard a croak so  now we have to specify a sample space  that involves the male frogs croaking  we know that there are three different  gender pairs that are possible male male  male female or female male  let's indicate a subscript of one if we  know that the frog croaks and zero if  the frog does not croak  we have to consider the sample space of  equally likely events  it could be that the first mill frog  croaked in a male male pair it could be  that the second male frog croaked in the  male male pair  or in the male and female pairs we would  have that the male frog is croaking  the video doesn't tell us actually  anything about the probability that male  and female frogs are croaking  so we're going to assume that these are  all equally likely events  the reason we have two different pairs  of male male frogs here is that either  frog could be croaking  so if we consider this sample space of  equally likely events the probability  that we have a female they're two  different events out of four so that  means the probability is equal to two  divided by four which is one-half  so summarizing the ted ed frog video  actually present they wanted to do a  calculation for variation a  what's the probability that there's a  female if you know that the pair  contains a male frog  this would be a probability of  two-thirds  what they actually presented  was the variation where you know there's  a mill frog because you heard a croak  that's distinctive to male frogs in that  case the probability becomes one half  and intuitively hearing the male croak  changes the sample space you're two  times more likely to hear a male croak  from a male male pair than from a female  male or a male female pair so i would  suggest the ted-ed frog video as  presented should have a probability of  one-half  i understand they wanted to present  variation a now the key in variation a  is that you hear this information by  random chance  now that's actually pretty hard to do in  practice as you can see that's where  they stumbled in the video and made the  mistake they included the detail you  have to learn it somehow in practice so  very very often in practice you're most  likely going to be presented with  variation b and that is going to be a  probability of one half  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books i've  written many books about probability and  game theory and similar topics there are  links in the video description

